    {
      "paper_id": "doi:10.1016/j.jue.2024.103708",
      "sequence": 9,
      "source_sequence": 9,
      "article_type": "research-article",
      "title_en": "Unlocking neighborhood density",
      "title_cn": "解锁街区密度",
      "authors": [
        "Melanie Krause",
        "André Seidel"
      ],
      "abstract_en": "Studying the components of neighborhood population density reveals a complex picture that little is known about. Hidden under the same level of population density, neighborhoods can vastly differ in crowding, if residential coverage or building heights are moving in opposite directions. We study this heterogeneity in density components and how it is linked to the variation in neighborhood socio-economic characteristics that define modern cities. To do so, we use novel high-resolution (10 m × 10 m) geo-spatial data on building height and footprints in combination with Norwegian register data. This data allows us to decompose the variation of density into its components, as well as along various margins. We identify urban spatial structures with a latent profile analysis. These data-driven density profiles turn out to be strongly associated with the sorting of people by socio-economic characteristics, such as income and demographic variables. Our results show that below the surface of density, there is the so-far unknown potential to learn about the prevalence and geography of socio-economic groups in the absence of micro-level data.",
      "abstract_cn": "研究街区人口密度的组成部分揭示了一幅鲜为人知的复杂图景。在相同的人口密度水平下，如果住宅覆盖率或建筑高度向相反方向变化，街区的拥挤程度可能大相径庭。我们研究了这种密度组成的异质性及其与定义现代城市的街区社会经济特征变化之间的联系。为此，我们使用了新颖的高分辨率（10米×10米）建筑高度和足迹地理空间数据，并结合挪威登记数据。这些数据使我们能够将密度的变化分解为其组成部分，以及沿各种边际的变化。我们通过潜在剖面分析识别城市空间结构。这些数据驱动的密度剖面结果与社会经济特征（如收入和人口统计变量）的人群分类密切相关。我们的结果表明，在密度的表面之下，存在迄今未知的潜力，可以在缺乏微观数据的情况下了解社会经济群体的分布和地理特征。",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.jue.2024.103708",
      "source_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/journal-of-urban-economics/vol/144/suppl/C",
      "publication_date": "November 2024",
